    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d307">

                <pb facs="d307-01" n="1" xml:id="d307-01"/>

                <head>307. Memorandum for the Record<anchor n="1" xml:id="d307-f1"/>
                    <anchor n="2" xml:id="d307-f2"/>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1971-12-03"
                        >December 3, 1971</date>
                </dateline>
                <p>General Haig has informed us that there is no objection in the White House to our
                    abstaining on all three test ban resolutions at the UN. General Haig said that
                    the NSC staff people concerned would be in touch with IO on the matter.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed> Robert H. Miller<lb/>Deputy Executive Secretary </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d307-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d307-f1-content">
                <p>Source: National Archives, RG 59, Central Files 1970–1973, DEF 18–4.
                    Confidential. Prepared by Robert H. Miller.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d307-f2" n="2" xml:id="d307-f2-content">
                <p>Deputy Assistant to the President for National Security Affairs Haig informed the
                    Department of State that the White House had no objection to it abstaining on
                    all three test ban resolutions before the UN.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
